what happens when you force it with explicit config

~/.config/opencode/opencode.json
```json
{
    "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
    "provider": {
        "google": {
            "name": "google",
            "options": {
                "baseURL": "https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta",
            },
            "models": {
                "models/gemma-4-26b-a4b-it": {
                    "name": "models/gemma-4-26b-a4b-it (google)",
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

~/.local/share/opencode/auth.json
```json
{
  "google": {
    "type": "api",
    "key": "..."
  }
}
```

then pick that model in the models drop-down, rather than the one autodetected from the provider models api call. (your path to these config files will be different on Windows)

I'm experiencing the same class of issue with `input_word_forward` and `input_word_backward`. Setting them to `"none"` or overriding them in `tui.json` has no effect — `alt+right`/`alt+left` still trigger word navigation.
**My use case:** Running OpenCode in IntelliJ's integrated terminal, where `alt+right`/`alt+left` are used for tab switching. OpenCode consumes these keys, preventing IntelliJ from handling them.
**tui.json config:**
```json
{
    "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/tui.json",
    "keybinds": {
        "input_word_forward": "alt+f",
        "input_word_backward": "alt+b"
    }
}
```
Setting `input_word_forward` to `"none"` also doesn't work — `alt+right` still moves the cursor by word.

Note: Below root cause analysis was performed by an AI assistant:

**Root cause investigation:**
The issue is in `@opentui/core`'s `Textarea` component ([`packages/core/src/renderables/Textarea.ts`](https://github.com/anomalyco/opentui/blob/main/packages/core/src/renderables/Textarea.ts)). It has hardcoded default keybindings:
```typescript
const defaultTextareaKeybindings: KeyBinding[] = [
  // ...
  { name: "right", meta: true, action: "word-forward" },
  { name: "left", meta: true, action: "word-backward" },
  { name: "right", ctrl: true, action: "word-forward" },
  { name: "left", ctrl: true, action: "word-backward" },
  // ...
]
```
These defaults are **always merged** with user-provided keybindings via `mergeKeyBindings()`:
```typescript
// In the constructor:
const mergedBindings = mergeKeyBindings(defaultTextareaKeybindings, this._keyBindings)
// In the keyBindings setter:
public set keyBindings(bindings: KeyBinding[]) {
    this._keyBindings = bindings
    const mergedBindings = mergeKeyBindings(defaultTextareaKeybindings, bindings)
    this._keyBindingsMap = buildKeyBindingsMap(mergedBindings, this._keyAliasMap)
}
```
So even though OpenCode's config layer in `packages/opencode/src/config/config.ts` correctly handles `"none"` (via `Keybind.parse("none")` returning `[]`), and `textarea-keybindings.ts` correctly maps the config to an empty binding list, the opentui Textarea always adds its own defaults back on top.
This affects all `input_*` keybinds, not just `input_submit` — it's the same root cause. The fix would need to happen in `@opentui/core`'s `mergeKeyBindings` to allow user bindings to fully replace (not just supplement) the defaults.

